\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Building the regression dataset of section~\ref {sec:dataset}, read from left to right. \emph  {States}: a uniformly random flow $a_1,\dots  ,a_L$ of \texttt  {ABC} primitives applied to the benchmark $\mathcal  {G}_0$ ends on a state $s_i$, an AIG functionally equivalent to $\mathcal  {G}_0$ but in general of a different size. The length $L$ is itself drawn at random, so the states sit at varying distances from $\mathcal  {G}_0$; three of the $1{,}000$ states of a dataset are drawn, with $L = 2$, $5$ and $7$. \emph  {Targets}: from each state, $M=100$ independent rollouts of eight further uniformly random primitives are run (grey; three are drawn, each dot being an AIG along the rollout), and the smallest of the AND counts $n^{(j)}(s_i)$ they end on is the target $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle V$}\mathaccent "0362{V}(s_i)$. The two dashed columns are the resulting dataset: every state is stored both as an \texttt  {.aig} file and as the statistics $x_\mathcal  {G}$ of section~\ref {sec:features} (features) and is paired with $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle V$}\mathaccent "0362{V}(s_i)$ (targets). One such dataset is built per benchmark and per seed.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dataset}{{3}{7}{Building the regression dataset of section~\ref {sec:dataset}, read from left to right. \emph {States}: a uniformly random flow $a_1,\dots ,a_L$ of \texttt {ABC} primitives applied to the benchmark $\mathcal {G}_0$ ends on a state $s_i$, an AIG functionally equivalent to $\mathcal {G}_0$ but in general of a different size. The length $L$ is itself drawn at random, so the states sit at varying distances from $\mathcal {G}_0$; three of the $1{,}000$ states of a dataset are drawn, with $L = 2$, $5$ and $7$. \emph {Targets}: from each state, $M=100$ independent rollouts of eight further uniformly random primitives are run (grey; three are drawn, each dot being an AIG along the rollout), and the smallest of the AND counts $n^{(j)}(s_i)$ they end on is the target $\widehat {V}(s_i)$. The two dashed columns are the resulting dataset: every state is stored both as an \texttt {.aig} file and as the statistics $x_\mathcal {G}$ of section~\ref {sec:features} (features) and is paired with $\widehat {V}(s_i)$ (targets). One such dataset is built per benchmark and per seed}{figure.caption.6}{}}
